            <ArticleTitle>Cost effectiveness of abciximab during routine medical practice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>265-74</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To estimate the cost per ischaemic event (death, nonfatal myocardial infarction, subsequent revascularisation procedure) avoided at 6 months in high risk patients undergoing coronary revascularisation treated with abciximab during routine medical care.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective, matched cohort design.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">62 abciximab-treated patients and 62 patients not treated with abciximab with high risk coronary lesions were matched according to gender, hyperlipidaemia, diabetes mellitus and stenting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Using a third-party payer's perspective, an incremental cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER) was computed as the cost per ischaemic event avoided over 6 months. Fieller's theorem was used to estimate confidence sets and confidence ellipses were generated to visually represent the variability in the data.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">At 6 months, abciximab-treated patients experienced an approximately 40% lower rate of ischaemic events (16.1 vs 27.4%; p = 0.128). The point estimate of the ICER was $US21,789 per ischaemic event avoided. Fieller's theorem resulted in a 95% confidence set consisting of 2 half-lines (-infinity to -$US115,461) and ($US391 to +infinity), reflecting the finding that the ICER denominator was not significantly different from zero at the p = 0.05 level.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In high risk patients treated during routine care, the effectiveness of abciximab was consistent with efficacy rates from clinical trials. However, abciximab-treated patients remained approximately $US2400 more costly at 6 months.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
